h the job opening number or job title. This will help direct hiring managers to the intended information. All files stored in the Cover Letter and Attachment section can be viewed by the hiring managers for every job for which you apply. Q: Why do the applications include a question about eligibility to work in the United States? A: The university must comply with the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986. Therefore, before you can begin work at the university you must present documents that prove your employment eligibility. If you have questions regarding acceptable documentation, please contact Human Resource Services. Q: What is voluntary self-identification, and why am I asked to complete the information? A: The human resources department maintains information regarding the diversity of the individuals who apply for work at the university. The information does not affect your prospects for employment. It is not shared with hiring managers and is treated confidentially. Q: What if I need special assistance? A: If you have special needs as defined by the Americans with Disabilities Act, notify human resources at the address listed on the web site, or call 573-341-4241during business hours. TTY users, call 1-800-735-2966. Q: What if I forget my password? A: There is an online password request process. Your password will be emailed to you if you request it. For other questions about your user name and password, contact the IT Helpdesk at 573-341-HELP (4357). Q: How long will it take for my application to be processed? A: In most cases applications are sent to the hiring department within two (2) business days of submission. Q: When will I find out whether I get an interview? A: Interviews are scheduled by hiring departments and are determined by several factors. It may be several weeks after the job is posted before interviews are scheduled. Q: I have an interview, where do I go? A: If you are contacted by phone for an interview, be sure to write down the name